   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.2. Increased capacity of Member States to ensure safe and healthy working conditions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">To improve effective implementation of the Law No.6331 on Occupational Health and Safety and relevant regulations, a two-pillar strategy has been implemented. The first pillar of the strategy targeted the vulnerable groups, including refugees and women. The second pillar targeted disadvantaged sectors, such as delivery and construction. The strategy also prioritised the due diligence to support social compliance. In this respect, collaboration with provincial administrations, municipalities, and NGOs was fostered addressing OSH rights and responsibilities. One significant achievement is strengthened capacity of employers, workers and government officials related to health examinations before recruitment process and periodic health examinations, including for workplaces with less than 10 employees. 
Provincial administrations, municipalities, NGOs and labour market representatives agreed on policy recommendations and immediate implementation actions on improving occupational safety and health for domestic and agricultural workers, including prioritisation of the trainings to be given to the domestic and agricultural workers by the Municipalities. Following this agreement, Municipalities started a campaign to increase the public awareness on the OSH needs in the said sectors. With the support of the Government of the United States, the ILO designed OSH trainings which were provided to vulnerable groups, such as refugees and women and health professionals, and in high-risk sectors.
Collaborating with local partners, ILO prepared sectoral OSH analyses and results. Stakeholders were empowered with evidence for informed decision-making, optimizing the impact of the strategy. The ILO prepared &quot;Focus on Motorcycle Couriers: Psychosocial Risk Analysis in Delivery Sector Employees&quot; Report that analyses the psychosocial risks arising from the content and context of work in the delivery sector. The report offers suggestions for the re-evaluation of the profession, which has become more visible with the pandemic, based on &quot;decent work&quot;.
The ILO, in collaboration with The Turkish Employers&apos; Association of Construction Industries, prepared and disseminated the &quot;Social Compliance Booklets on Construction Sector&quot;. The booklets involve step-by-step guide for effective implementation of OSH policies in construction sector.
The ILO conducted sectoral workshops on occupational safety and health of domestic workers and agricultural workers which convened provincial administrations, municipalities, NGOs and labour market representatives, fostering partnerships for coordinated action on responding to the sectoral OSH needs of domestic and agricultural workers. 
The ILO, together with the Directorate of Guidance and Inspection under Ministry of Labour and Social Security, prepared a new project on &quot;Workplace Compliance through Labour Inspection Guidance and Social Dialogue&quot; to be funded by European Union. The project is approved, and the contract is signed as of March 2023. This newly started project will enable the ILO to support further the operationalisation of Law on OSH in T&#xfc;rkiye.
Following the earthquakes, the ILO developed information notes on OSH risks that arise due to asbestos in the region, with an aim to raise awareness of public.</narrative>
